 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S443-018\",\n\"clauses\": {\n\"count_multiples\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"F11 is the only question_text family that asks the learner to continue a counted sequence. Its seq slot draws from F07 and F12, whose terms are generated from direction, sequence and skip_by, so the family produces counting-on problems across the declared skip_pool.\"},\n\"twos\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"F07 members include 948, 950, 952, 954 and F12 members include 482, 484, 486, 634, 636, so F11 can print a 2s run with skip_by 2.\"},\n\"fives\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"F07 members include 150, 155, 160, 165 and 445, 450, 455, 460, so F11 can print a 5s run.\"},\n\"tens\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"F07 members include 190, 200, 210, 220 and 720, 730, 740, 750, so F11 can print a 10s run.\"},\n\"twenties\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"F07 members include 40, 60, 80, 100 and 800, 820, 840, so F11 can print a 20s run.\"},\n\"fifties\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"F07 members include 150, 200, 250, 300 and 400, 450, 500, so F11 can print a 50s run.\"},\n\"hundreds\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"F07 members include 100, 200, 300, 400 and 600, 700, 800, so F11 can print a 100s run.\"},\n\"not_beyond_1000\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"The declared range is 10 to 1000, and F11 sequences drawn from the same families can run up to the top of that range. Observed F07 members reach 954 and the F08 answer runs reach 1000, which is a hint, not a question, so the evidence for the upper bound in question text is thin.\"}\n},\n\"failing_families\": [\"F14\"],\n\"failing_templates\": [\"What number is marked{suffix}?\"],\n\"overall\": \"FAIL\",\n\"reasoning\": \"F14 prints the question 'What number is marked{suffix}?' for every problem of this lc, and its suffix domain is fixed to the vocabulary-gated phrase for number line, so every draw asks the learner to read a marked point. The competency names counting by 2s, 5s, 10s, 20s, 50s and 100s, and nothing about reading positions on a number line. The question names no skip and no counting, and no clause of this lc is addressed by it, so it is content outside the competency's explicit scope (Rule 1, Content Rule 3). Its vocabulary (number line) is cumulative, so the vocabulary gate itself is not at fault. The other families do not fail. F01 prints the gated word jumping by, which is not in NOT_YET_KNOWN. F02 prints list of numbers. F03, F05, F06 and F08 are hints whose values are consistent with skip_by and with sequence-start arithmetic in the observed renders (for example, observed last and answer pairs such as 62 with 64 and 995 with 1000 differ by the skip). F04, F07, F09, F10, F11 and F12 print counting sequences with no NOT_YET_KNOWN terms. The F06 action and prep slots are both keyed to direction, so they are treated as coupled. F07 and F09 include 0 among the members, which sits below the declared 10 to 1000 range; this is noted but not counted as a failure because counting from 0 is standard for these skips and the member list is the declared domain.\"}",
